      "id": "lines",
      "title": "Piano lines: decay, ending and voice",
      "question": "Scale, repeated notes, a legato line, a fast run and a long C4: which sounds most like a piano imitation?",
      "notes": "Now: a note is gone 1.4 s after it starts (at C4; Gervill's piano is still only 29 dB down at 3 s) and closes on 'ng' when the key lifts. 'Longer' rings about as long as Gervill's (decay 2.5 s at C4, a little shorter up high). 'No ng' keeps the vowel as it is while the note fades. 'Steady e' sings 'e' all through instead of drifting from 'i' to 'e' (measured: the drift makes some notes swell or dip by 5-10 dB as they ring, since the moving vowel crosses the harmonics). 'Bigger voice' is voice size 0.4 instead of 0.6 (a larger, darker head). One line, so the vibrato lock makes no difference here. Every clip at Gervill's loudness.",
